      "text": "To determine which functions have inverses, we need to check if each function is one-to-one. A function has an inverse if and only if it is strictly monotonic (either strictly increasing or strictly decreasing). Let's analyze each graph:\n\n1. **Graph A:**\n   The graph is a cubic function. It is not strictly monotonic over its entire domain. It has local maxima and minima, so it is not one-to-one. Therefore, its inverse does not exist.\n\n2. **Graph B:**\n   The graph passes the horizontal line test, meaning it is one-to-one. It is a parabola opening upwards. Therefore, its inverse exists.\n\n3. **Graph C:**\n   The graph passes the horizontal line test, meaning it is one-to-one. It is a semicircle. Therefore, its inverse exists.\n\n4. **Graph D:**\n   The graph is a circle centered at the origin with a radius of 2. It is not one-to-one over its entire domain because it is symmetric about the y-axis. Therefore, its inverse does not exist.\n\n5. **Graph E:**\n   The function is a cubic polynomial. To determine if it is one-to-one, we can examine its derivative:\n   \\[\n   f'(x) = \\frac{3x^2 + 2x - 2}{40}\n   \\]\n   The derivative \\( f'(x) \\) is always positive for all \\( x \\) because the quadratic \\( 3x^2 + 2x - 2 \\) has a positive leading coefficient and its discriminant \\( 2^2 - 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot (-2) = 4 + 24 = 28 \\) is positive, indicating two distinct real roots. Therefore, \\( f(x) \\) is strictly increasing, and its inverse exists.\n\nSo, the graphs that have inverses are B, C, and E.\n\nThe answer is \\(\\boxed{B,C,E}\\).",
